/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  compaction01 -- MICROCRYSTALLINE CELLULOSE ON THE COMPACTION BENCH.

  MCC is the main excipient of the apalutamide tablet case
  (tutorials/batch/plant/apalutamide01_tablets: MCC 295.5 + silicified MCC
  116.9 mg of a 700 mg core).  This case fits the three compaction models
  to OPEN measurements of MCC, so the numbers a tablet-press design needs --
  how hard the press must push, how much the bed shrinks, how strong the
  tablet comes out -- have a source a reader can open.

  WHY A NEW FOLDER, `tutorials/props/powder/`.  The tutorial folders of
  `props/` are named by the subject a student studies, and powder
  compaction is none of the existing ones (it is not thermodynamics,
  transport or a membrane): it is solids processing, the first props bench
  of its kind.

  THREE OPERATIONS, TWO SOURCES (both CC BY 4.0; datasets in
  constant/experimental/, each with its own provenance header):

    mcc_inDie            Avicel PH200, van der Haven et al., Sci. Data 13
                         (2026) 1468 -- 45 tablets compressed once on a
                         compaction simulator, IN-DIE density at maximum
                         force; 22 tablets crushed diametrally.  Heckel on
                         the declared low-pressure window, Kawakita on all
                         pressures, Ryshkewitch-Duckworth on the crushing
                         data, compared with the sigma_0 and k_b the dataset
                         itself reports.
    mcc_inDie_highWindow the SAME 45 tablets, Heckel on a high-pressure
                         window: the mean yield pressure roughly doubles.
                         The window IS part of the answer, which is why the
                         case declares it and the op never chooses it.
    mcc_lubricated       CEOLUS UF-711 + 2 % sodium stearyl fumarate + 0.3 %
                         silica (Kukuls & Mohylyuk 2025 dataset; protocol of
                         Mohylyuk et al., Pharmaceutics 16 (2024) 362), the
                         mean of ten tablets at each of six pressures, OUT OF
                         DIE, compared with the Py = 106 MPa that paper
                         reports.

  WHAT TO READ IN THE OUTPUT.  Py of MCC is low (tens to ~100 MPa in die on
  the low-pressure line): it deforms PLASTICALLY, which is why it binds so
  well and why it is the filler of choice for direct compression.  Compare
  it with compaction02 (dicalcium phosphate, brittle, several hundred MPa).
  The out-of-die Py is HIGHER than the in-die one on similar pressures
  because the ejected tablet has recovered the elastic part of its strain.

  THE PAPER'S Py IS NOT REPRODUCED, AND THE REASON IS THE LESSON.  Mohylyuk
  et al. 2024 state their linear region as 100-150 MPa.  The deposited data
  carries ONE of its six pressures in that window (137 MPa), and a line
  through fewer than 4 points refuses here, so this case declares 90-340
  MPa (4 points) and gets a different, larger, Py.  (The two-point slope
  between the 91 and 137 MPa rows would give about 100 MPa -- close to the
  paper's -- but a two-point line has no residual to judge it by.)  Both
  numbers are printed; nothing is tuned.

  A FINDING, PRINTED AND NOT TUNED: on both materials of the van der Haven
  dataset (here and in the other powder case) the fitted k_b agrees with
  the dataset's own constant within 10 %, but the fitted sigma_0 comes out
  about 1.5 times the dataset's.  A fit in sigma_t instead of ln(sigma_t)
  does not close it (measured: 30.9 and 28.4 MPa).  The files read do not
  say how the dataset's "Eq. 4" was fitted or on which porosity; its report
  PDF was not read.  sigma_0 is an extrapolation to zero porosity, so a
  small difference in slope or in the porosity basis moves it a lot.

  HOW TO USE THIS FOR YOUR FORMULATION.
    1. Find an OPEN dataset (CC BY: MDPI Pharmaceutics, Int. J. Pharm. X,
       Sci. Data, Zenodo, institutional dataverses) for the excipient that
       dominates your blend -- the same grade if you can.  Check its licence
       on the article page; NonCommercial or "available on request" is not
       usable.
    2. Write its rows in the experiments grammar (copy one of the files in
       constant/experimental/): P in MPa and ONE of D, porosity or the
       compact's density (then declare `trueDensity`, with its source).
       Say whether the density is in-die or out-of-die.
    3. Plot the Heckel line first (run with a wide window and look at the
       `heckel` table's residuals), then DECLARE the linear region you
       defend, with a `reason`.  Report Py WITH its window and its in-die
       or out-of-die basis; a Py without them cannot be compared.
    4. If the source reports its own values, put them in `reported {}`.
       A difference is a finding (window, density basis, true density),
       never something to tune away.
  LIMITS, said plainly: these are data for MCC, not for your blend.  NO
  mixture rule (for Py, sigma_0 or k_b) is implemented; the source's own
  ternary data shows mixtures do not follow a simple average.  Lubricant
  (magnesium stearate) weakens MCC tablets and the lubricated dataset here
  is the closer stand-in for a real formulation.  For another MCC grade the
  literature gives in-die Py = 52.2 +/- 4.2 MPa for Vivapur 102 at 11.28 mm
  (Lura et al., Int. J. Pharm. X 2 (2020) 100061, Table 2, CC BY) -- quoted
  for comparison, not fitted here.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name        mcc_inDie;
        type        compactionFit;
        material    "MCC Avicel PH200 (in-die, van der Haven et al. 2026)";
        dataset     "constant/experimental/vanderHaven2026_mcc_inDie.dat";
        //  The dataset's own EFFECTIVE true density (Kuentz-Leuenberger fit,
        //  Processed data/Effective true density/Effective true densities.tab).
        trueDensity 1.6374 g/cm3;

        heckel
        {
            Pmin    30 MPa;
            Pmax    170 MPa;
            reason  "the low-pressure line where plastic flow dominates: below ~30 MPa the bed is still rearranging, above ~170 MPa D > 0.88 and the in-die curve bends towards D = 1";
        }
        kawakita
        {
            Pmin    15 MPa;
            Pmax    700 MPa;
            reason  "P/C is straight over the whole range the dataset covers";
        }
        ryshkewitch
        {
            dataset "constant/experimental/vanderHaven2026_mcc_diametral.dat";
        }
        reported
        {
            sigma0  21.7 MPa;
            kb      6.6;
            source  "van der Haven et al. 2026, Zenodo archive, Processed data/Single/Ryshkewitch-Duckworth constants.csv, 100 wt% MCC, diametrical";
        }
    }
    {
        name        mcc_inDie_highWindow;
        type        compactionFit;
        material    "MCC Avicel PH200 (in-die, van der Haven et al. 2026), high-pressure window";
        dataset     "constant/experimental/vanderHaven2026_mcc_inDie.dat";
        trueDensity 1.6374 g/cm3;
        heckel
        {
            Pmin    250 MPa;
            Pmax    450 MPa;
            reason  "deliberately the HIGH-pressure end, to show how much Py depends on the window";
        }
    }
    {
        name        mcc_lubricated;
        type        compactionFit;
        material    "MCC CEOLUS UF-711 + 2 % PRUV + 0.3 % Syloid (out-of-die, Kukuls & Mohylyuk 2025)";
        dataset     "constant/experimental/kukuls2025_F_100-0.dat";
        heckel
        {
            Pmin    90 MPa;
            Pmax    340 MPa;
            reason  "the four lowest of six pressures: the solid fraction stops rising above ~340 MPa (89.3, 90.0, 89.7 %); the paper's own 100-150 MPa window holds one deposited row";
        }
        kawakita
        {
            Pmin    90 MPa;
            Pmax    480 MPa;
            reason  "all six main compressions (in-die strain)";
        }
        ryshkewitch { }
        reported
        {
            Py      106 MPa;
            source  "Mohylyuk et al., Pharmaceutics 16 (2024) 362, section 3 (out-of-die Heckel, F 100-0; linear region 100-150 MPa)";
        }
    }
);
